INT. LAUNDROMAT PAYPHONE ALCOVE - NIGHT

Humming dryers. A bank of washers behind a glass wall. A payphone on a tile wall, its cord scarred by years of nervous fingers.

KENDRA ABERNATHY-VOSS (30s, hospital badge on a lanyard, hair wet from the rain) feeds coins into the slot. She dials with trembling care.

The phone rings twice. A click.

FATHER (V.O.)
(through the receiver, gruff)
Yeah.

KENDRA
Dad. It's me.

FATHER (V.O.)
Kendra. Thought you'd be asleep.

KENDRA
I'm on nights this week.

FATHER (V.O.)
Right. Right, nights. They work you too hard.

KENDRA
It's fine.

FATHER (V.O.)
You eating?

KENDRA
Yes.

FATHER (V.O.)
Real food?

KENDRA
Dad.

FATHER (V.O.)
Okay, okay.

Silence. On the line, a television murmurs in another room. She winds the metal cord around her finger until the tip turns white.

KENDRA
How's Mom?

FATHER (V.O.)
She's good. Good. She's in bed.

KENDRA
She sleeping?

FATHER (V.O.)
She's, uh. Yeah. She's sleeping.

KENDRA
Dad.

FATHER (V.O.)
What?

KENDRA
You're doing the voice.

FATHER (V.O.)
What voice?

KENDRA
The one you used when Buster died.

A long pause on the line. The television fades out. Then a breath, shaky.

FATHER (V.O.)
Honey, I was going to call you.

KENDRA
When?

FATHER (V.O.)
In the morning. After you slept.

Kendra rests her forehead against the cold tile. A dryer buzzes behind her. She doesn't turn.

KENDRA
What time?

FATHER (V.O.)
About nine o'clock. Just after the nurses.

KENDRA
I'm coming.

FATHER (V.O.)
Honey, there's nothing to...

KENDRA
I'm coming, Dad.

She hangs the receiver on its hook, very gently, as though it were asleep. Stands there a long moment. Then she goes to the glass wall, opens a dryer, and starts folding warm towels one by one.

FADE OUT.
